538
00:20:54,042 --> 00:21:00,042
The major key to your better future is you.

539
00:21:00,042 --> 00:21:02,542
For a big share of my life now,

540
00:21:02,542 --> 00:21:05,042
I didn't have this one quite figured out.

541
00:21:05,042 --> 00:21:06,542
Among a lot of things

542
00:21:06,542 --> 00:21:08,042
I didn't have quite figured out.

543
00:21:08,042 --> 00:21:09,542
Many things used to puzzle

544
00:21:09,542 --> 00:21:11,042
me back in those early days.

545
00:21:11,042 --> 00:21:12,542
I used to wonder why two

546
00:21:12,542 --> 00:21:14,042
people could work for the same company.

547
00:21:14,042 --> 00:21:16,042
One make twice as much money.

548
00:21:17,042 --> 00:21:19,042
Now see that used to puzzle me.

549
00:21:19,042 --> 00:21:21,042
And maybe they were the same age.

550
00:21:21,042 --> 00:21:23,042
Graduated from the same school.

551
00:21:23,042 --> 00:21:24,042
Live in the same community.

552
00:21:24,042 --> 00:21:25,042
Work for the same company.

553
00:21:25,042 --> 00:21:28,042
With the same products and the same services.

554
00:21:28,042 --> 00:21:30,042
They've got the same traffic.

555
00:21:30,042 --> 00:21:31,042
The same problems.

556
00:21:31,042 --> 00:21:33,042
And one makes a thousand a month.

557
00:21:33,042 --> 00:21:36,042
The other one makes two thousand a month.

558
00:21:36,042 --> 00:21:38,042
Now that was my puzzling question.

559
00:21:38,042 --> 00:21:40,042
Why would this long list be the

560
00:21:40,042 --> 00:21:42,042
same and the money twice as much?

561
00:21:42,042 --> 00:21:44,042
I asked, what's the difference between a

562
00:21:44,042 --> 00:21:46,042
thousand a month and two thousand a month?

563
00:21:47,042 --> 00:21:49,042
And I don't mean a thousand a month, right?

564
00:21:49,042 --> 00:21:50,042
I could figure that out.

565
00:21:50,042 --> 00:21:52,042
But what makes the difference?

566
00:21:52,042 --> 00:21:54,042
Why would one person do twice

567
00:21:54,042 --> 00:21:56,042
as well, three times as well?

568
00:21:56,042 --> 00:21:57,042
Speaking economically.

569
00:21:57,042 --> 00:21:58,042
Now I know there's more

570
00:21:58,042 --> 00:21:59,042
than one way to do well.

571
00:21:59,042 --> 00:22:00,042
I understand that.

572
00:22:00,042 --> 00:22:03,042
But in this little narrow

573
00:22:03,042 --> 00:22:06,042
area called compensation, what's the difference?

574
00:22:06,042 --> 00:22:08,542
Well back then with my faulty

575
00:22:08,542 --> 00:22:11,042
thinking, I'm trying to reason it out.

576
00:22:11,042 --> 00:22:12,542
I thought, well maybe time

577
00:22:12,542 --> 00:22:14,042
makes some of the difference, right?

578
00:22:14,042 --> 00:22:16,042
Some people do better because they have more time.

579
00:22:17,042 --> 00:22:18,542
I used to say, Harold ought

580
00:22:18,542 --> 00:22:20,042
to be able to do well.

581
00:22:20,042 --> 00:22:22,042
He's got a lot of time.

582
00:22:22,042 --> 00:22:24,042
If I had all of

583
00:22:24,042 --> 00:22:26,042
Harold's time, I could do well.

584
00:22:26,042 --> 00:22:29,042
Now that's got to be dumb, right?

585
00:22:29,042 --> 00:22:34,042
Number one, you can't get somebody else's time.

586
00:22:34,042 --> 00:22:36,042
A guy says to me one time, he says, you know,

587
00:22:36,042 --> 00:22:38,042
if I had some extra time, I could make some extra money.

588
00:22:38,042 --> 00:22:39,042
I said, then forget it.

589
00:22:39,042 --> 00:22:42,042
There isn't any extra time.

590
00:22:42,042 --> 00:22:43,542
Hey, when the clock strikes 12

591
00:22:43,542 --> 00:22:45,042
midnight, that about wraps it up, right?

592
00:22:45,042 --> 00:22:47,042
I mean, you can look around the gongs around here.

593
00:22:47,042 --> 00:22:50,042
I'm here for a little more, but it's over.

594
00:22:50,042 --> 00:22:51,042
You say to the guy, what are you doing?

595
00:22:51,042 --> 00:22:52,042
He said, I'm looking for extra time.

596
00:22:52,042 --> 00:22:55,042
See, they'll come and take you away, right?

597
00:22:55,042 --> 00:22:59,042
There isn't any more time.

598
00:22:59,042 --> 00:23:02,542
Now, if you can't get more time, which you can't, what could

599
00:23:02,542 --> 00:23:06,042
you get more of that would make a difference in economic results?

600
00:23:06,042 --> 00:23:08,042
And here's the key word.

601
00:23:08,042 --> 00:23:09,042
Make it a part of your notes.

602
00:23:09,042 --> 00:23:11,042
We're going to consider it tonight.

603
00:23:11,042 --> 00:23:15,042
The word is value.

604
00:23:15,042 --> 00:23:17,042
And I have a little phrase for your notes.

605
00:23:17,042 --> 00:23:21,042
Value makes the difference in results.

606
00:23:21,042 --> 00:23:24,042
Value makes the difference.

607
00:23:24,042 --> 00:23:27,042
You can't get more time,

608
00:23:27,042 --> 00:23:30,042
but you can create more value.

609
00:23:30,042 --> 00:23:35,042
Now, here's the first lesson of economics.

610
00:23:35,042 --> 00:23:36,542
Everybody should learn it from the time they're

611
00:23:36,542 --> 00:23:38,042
old enough to understand what a dollar means.

612
00:23:38,042 --> 00:23:39,542
How to earn one, how to get one,

613
00:23:39,542 --> 00:23:41,042
how to keep one, what to do with it.

614
00:23:41,042 --> 00:23:44,042
First lesson of economics.

615
00:23:44,042 --> 00:23:47,042
We primarily get paid for value.

616
00:23:47,042 --> 00:23:50,042
That's lesson one.

617
00:23:50,042 --> 00:23:52,042
Bringing value to the marketplace.

618
00:23:52,042 --> 00:23:54,042
That's how you get paid.

619
00:23:54,042 --> 00:23:56,042
You don't get paid for the time.

620
00:23:56,042 --> 00:24:00,042
I know it takes time to bring value to the

621
00:24:00,042 --> 00:24:04,042
marketplace, but you get paid for the value, not the time.

622
00:24:04,042 --> 00:24:05,542
Now, since that's true, here's one

623
00:24:05,542 --> 00:24:07,042
of the key questions of the evening.

624
00:24:07,042 --> 00:24:10,542
Is it possible to become

625
00:24:10,542 --> 00:24:14,042
twice as valuable at the marketplace

626
00:24:14,042 --> 00:24:16,042
and make twice as much money in the store?

627
00:24:17,042 --> 00:24:20,042
At the same time?

628
00:24:20,042 --> 00:24:24,042
Could you become three times as valuable?

629
00:24:24,042 --> 00:24:27,042
Make three times as much money in the same time?

630
00:24:27,042 --> 00:24:29,042
Is that possible?

631
00:24:29,042 --> 00:24:33,042
The answer is yes, if.

632
00:24:33,042 --> 00:24:35,042
And it's always if, right?

633
00:24:35,042 --> 00:24:39,042
Life is known as the big if.

634
00:24:39,042 --> 00:24:41,042
Harry Truman once said,

635
00:24:41,042 --> 00:24:43,042
Life is iffy.

636
00:24:43,042 --> 00:24:45,042
How true.

637
00:24:45,042 --> 00:24:46,042
And here's the big if.

638
00:24:46,042 --> 00:24:47,042
We're going to consider it tonight.

639
00:24:47,042 --> 00:24:51,042
It's possible to do much better at the

640
00:24:51,042 --> 00:24:55,042
marketplace if you go to work primarily on yourself.

641
00:24:55,042 --> 00:24:58,042
And that's the theme of our seminar tonight.

642
00:24:58,042 --> 00:25:06,042
Learning to work primarily on yourself.

643
00:25:06,042 --> 00:25:09,042
People have asked me for the last 24 years,

644
00:25:09,042 --> 00:25:12,042
How do you develop an above average income?

645
00:25:12,042 --> 00:25:13,042
And the answer is,

646
00:25:13,042 --> 00:25:15,042
Become an above average person.

647
00:25:16,042 --> 00:25:20,042
Develop an above average handshake.

648
00:25:20,042 --> 00:25:22,042
Some people want to be successful,

649
00:25:22,042 --> 00:25:24,042
they don't even work on their handshake.

650
00:25:24,042 --> 00:25:26,042
As easy as that would be to start on.

651
00:25:26,042 --> 00:25:28,042
They let it slide, they don't understand.

652
00:25:28,042 --> 00:25:31,042
Develop an above average smile.

653
00:25:31,042 --> 00:25:34,042
Develop an above average excitement.

654
00:25:34,042 --> 00:25:36,042
Develop an above average interest in other people.

655
00:25:36,042 --> 00:25:39,042
Develop an above average intensity to win.

656
00:25:39,042 --> 00:25:41,042
See, that'll change everything.

657
00:25:41,042 --> 00:25:45,042
Probably one of the most frustrating experiences in my life.

658
00:25:45,042 --> 00:25:49,042
Is looking for an above average job with

659
00:25:49,042 --> 00:25:53,042
above average pay without becoming an above average person.

660
00:25:53,042 --> 00:25:55,042
It's called frustration.

661
00:25:55,042 --> 00:26:00,042
And Mr. Shouf gave me probably the greatest

662
00:26:00,042 --> 00:26:05,042
clue he gave me when I first met him.

663
00:26:05,042 --> 00:26:09,042
He said, Jim, if you want to be wealthy and

664
00:26:09,042 --> 00:26:13,042
happy the rest of your life, just learn this lesson well.

665
00:26:13,042 --> 00:26:17,042
He said, learn to work harder on

666
00:26:17,042 --> 00:26:21,042
yourself than you do on your job.

667
00:26:21,042 --> 00:26:26,042
That was it.

668
00:26:26,042 --> 00:26:27,542
The beginning of the philosophy

669
00:26:27,542 --> 00:26:29,042
that helped to change my life.

670
00:26:29,042 --> 00:26:32,042
Work harder on yourself than you do on your job.

671
00:26:32,042 --> 00:26:35,042
When he said that, I

672
00:26:35,042 --> 00:26:38,042
suddenly understood why I was broke.

673
00:26:38,042 --> 00:26:42,042
I was a hard worker.

674
00:26:43,042 --> 00:26:46,042
On my job.

675
00:26:46,042 --> 00:26:48,042
If anybody would have asked me, age

676
00:26:48,042 --> 00:26:50,042
25, Mr. Rohn, are you a hard worker?

677
00:26:50,042 --> 00:26:52,042
I'd have said, yes, I'm a hard worker.

678
00:26:52,042 --> 00:26:53,042
Check my record.

679
00:26:53,042 --> 00:26:55,042
I work as hard as anybody.

680
00:26:55,042 --> 00:26:56,042
And that was true.

681
00:26:56,042 --> 00:26:57,042
I was a hard worker.

682
00:26:57,042 --> 00:26:58,042
I really was.

683
00:26:58,042 --> 00:27:00,042
But see, that was my problem.

684
00:27:00,042 --> 00:27:02,542
I was working hard on my job,

685
00:27:02,542 --> 00:27:05,042
but I wasn't working hard on myself.

686
00:27:05,042 --> 00:27:08,042
And that was my reason for lack of progress.

687
00:27:08,042 --> 00:27:11,542
Some people look for more money, but

688
00:27:11,542 --> 00:27:15,042
they look for it in the wrong direction.

689
00:27:15,042 --> 00:27:17,042
The guy says, I need more money.

690
00:27:17,042 --> 00:27:19,042
I'm going to go work on my boss.

691
00:27:19,042 --> 00:27:26,042
Well, number one, bosses are notorious.

692
00:27:26,042 --> 00:27:27,542
They do not play fast

693
00:27:27,542 --> 00:27:29,042
and loose with the company till.

694
00:27:29,042 --> 00:27:30,542
I've never seen a boss

695
00:27:30,542 --> 00:27:32,042
get excited and triple somebody's wages.

696
00:27:32,042 --> 00:27:33,042
Right?

697
00:27:33,042 --> 00:27:35,042
I mean, they don't behave in such an irrational manner.

698
00:27:35,042 --> 00:27:37,042
So, see, that's not what you work on.

699
00:27:38,042 --> 00:27:42,042
Some people say, well, I'll go on strike for more.

700
00:27:42,042 --> 00:27:44,042
Well, big deal.

701
00:27:44,042 --> 00:27:46,042
The problem with that game is

702
00:27:46,042 --> 00:27:48,042
once you start it, it's never over.

703
00:27:48,042 --> 00:27:51,042
You'll always have to strike.

704
00:27:51,042 --> 00:27:53,042
And I'll tell you what you get by demand.

705
00:27:53,042 --> 00:27:56,042
Little bitty pieces.

706
00:27:56,042 --> 00:27:59,042
Barely enough.

707
00:27:59,042 --> 00:28:01,042
And now inflation beats that game.

708
00:28:01,042 --> 00:28:02,042
Right?

709
00:28:02,042 --> 00:28:03,042
The guy demands 10%.

710
00:28:03,042 --> 00:28:04,042
Inflation's 12.

711
00:28:04,042 --> 00:28:06,042
He's behind two.

712
00:28:06,042 --> 00:28:08,042
I mean, it never ends.

713
00:28:08,042 --> 00:28:11,042
So, see, that's not the key to success.

714
00:28:11,042 --> 00:28:15,042
Maybe survival, but not success.

715
00:28:15,042 --> 00:28:18,042
The guy in sales says, I've got it.

716
00:28:18,042 --> 00:28:20,042
I'll get me some of those

717
00:28:20,042 --> 00:28:22,042
books that teach you the tricky sales.

718
00:28:22,042 --> 00:28:24,042
And I'll go leap upon my

719
00:28:24,042 --> 00:28:26,042
prospects, dazzling with my sales footwork,

720
00:28:26,042 --> 00:28:28,042
grab their money before they know what's happened.

721
00:28:28,042 --> 00:28:29,542
Well, you can play those kind

722
00:28:29,542 --> 00:28:31,042
of tricks if you want to.

723
00:28:31,042 --> 00:28:34,042
My observation shows the last 24 years, you wind up

724
00:28:34,042 --> 00:28:37,042
at the bottom of the economic ladder, not the top.

725
00:28:38,042 --> 00:28:42,042
See, it's not what you get by tricks.

726
00:28:42,042 --> 00:28:45,042
It's not what you get by demand.

727
00:28:45,042 --> 00:28:50,042
It's what you get by performance that counts.

728
00:28:50,042 --> 00:28:52,042
So that's one of the key phrases of the evening.

729
00:28:52,042 --> 00:28:57,042
It's performance that counts.

730
00:28:57,042 --> 00:28:59,042
And I discovered this.

731
00:28:59,042 --> 00:29:04,042
Performance comes from inside, not outside.

732
00:29:04,042 --> 00:29:05,542
For a share of my life, I'm

733
00:29:05,542 --> 00:29:07,042
looking for the answers to do well outside.

734
00:29:07,042 --> 00:29:11,042
Found out they were inside.

735
00:29:18,042 --> 00:29:20,542
Then Mr. Shouff gave me probably

736
00:29:20,542 --> 00:29:23,042
one of the most important clues

737
00:29:23,042 --> 00:29:25,042
among so many things he taught me.

738
00:29:25,042 --> 00:29:27,042
But this was in those early days.

739
00:29:27,042 --> 00:29:29,542
Mr. Shouff was very kind,

740
00:29:29,542 --> 00:29:32,042
but he was also very abrupt.

741
00:29:32,042 --> 00:29:35,042
And he had these interesting questions to ask.

742
00:29:35,042 --> 00:29:37,042
I'm giving him a little run day.

743
00:29:37,042 --> 00:29:38,542
Run down one day on how

744
00:29:38,542 --> 00:29:40,042
things hadn't worked out for me.

745
00:29:40,042 --> 00:29:42,042
He said, Mr. Owen, I've got the

746
00:29:42,042 --> 00:29:44,042
answer for you if you will listen carefully.

747
00:29:44,042 --> 00:29:46,042
And listen carefully I did that

748
00:29:46,042 --> 00:29:48,042
day and for the next five years.

749
00:29:48,042 --> 00:29:54,042
If somebody's wealthy and happy, you gotta listen.

750
00:29:54,042 --> 00:29:57,042
He said, Jim, I've only known you a short time.

751
00:29:57,042 --> 00:30:00,542
But he said, it's already my honest opinion that

752
00:30:00,542 --> 00:30:04,042
for things to change for you, you got to change.

753
00:30:04,042 --> 00:30:06,042
That wasn't quite the answer.

754
00:30:07,042 --> 00:30:10,042
That wasn't quite the answer I was looking for.

755
00:30:10,042 --> 00:30:12,042
But that's the answer he gave me.

756
00:30:12,042 --> 00:30:14,542
And I pass it along to you

757
00:30:14,542 --> 00:30:17,042
on this warm summer evening in Anaheim, California.

758
00:30:19,042 --> 00:30:26,042
For things to change for you, you've got to change.

759
00:30:26,042 --> 00:30:30,042
Otherwise, it isn't gonna change.

760
00:30:30,042 --> 00:30:33,042
Before I met Mr. Shouff, I used

761
00:30:33,042 --> 00:30:36,042
to say, I sure hope things will change.

762
00:30:36,042 --> 00:30:37,042
Right?

763
00:30:37,042 --> 00:30:39,042
That seemed to be my only hope.

764
00:30:39,042 --> 00:30:42,042
If it isn't gonna change, I'm in serious trouble.

765
00:30:42,042 --> 00:30:44,042
And then I discovered it isn't gonna change.

766
00:30:44,042 --> 00:30:46,042
So I'm in serious trouble.

767
00:30:46,042 --> 00:30:50,542
See, I can tell you what

768
00:30:50,542 --> 00:30:55,042
the 80s are gonna be like.

769
00:30:55,042 --> 00:30:58,042
You have dropped into the right place.

770
00:30:58,042 --> 00:31:01,542
I did a seminar one time for

771
00:31:01,542 --> 00:31:05,042
Standard Oil executives and management in Honolulu.

772
00:31:06,042 --> 00:31:08,042
And we're having a conference one

773
00:31:08,042 --> 00:31:10,042
day on this big conference table.

774
00:31:10,042 --> 00:31:12,042
And one of them said to me, Mr. Rohn,

775
00:31:12,042 --> 00:31:14,042
you know some fairly important people halfway around the world.

776
00:31:14,042 --> 00:31:16,042
What do you think the 80s are gonna be like?

777
00:31:16,042 --> 00:31:19,042
I said, gentlemen, I do know the right people.

778
00:31:19,042 --> 00:31:21,042
I can tell you.

779
00:31:21,042 --> 00:31:24,042
So they all listened very carefully.

780
00:31:24,042 --> 00:31:27,042
And I said, gentlemen, based on my wide experience,

781
00:31:27,042 --> 00:31:30,042
I can really honestly say to you, in my opinion,

782
00:31:30,042 --> 00:31:32,542
in the 80s, it's gonna

783
00:31:32,542 --> 00:31:35,042
be about like it's always been.

784
00:31:36,042 --> 00:31:38,042
Aren't you glad you came?

785
00:31:38,042 --> 00:31:39,042
That's inside.

786
00:31:39,042 --> 00:31:43,042
I don't pass that around just everywhere.

787
00:31:43,042 --> 00:31:45,042
Now, of course, I said that to make a

788
00:31:45,042 --> 00:31:47,042
point, but I also said it because it's accurate.

789
00:31:47,042 --> 00:31:50,042
It's gonna be about like it's always been.

790
00:31:50,042 --> 00:31:53,042
It isn't gonna change.

791
00:31:53,042 --> 00:31:55,042
The tide comes in and then what?

792
00:31:55,042 --> 00:31:58,542
It goes out for six and a half thousand years

793
00:31:58,542 --> 00:32:02,042
that we know of, recorded history, and probably long before that.

794
00:32:02,042 --> 00:32:04,042
So it is not gonna change.

795
00:32:04,042 --> 00:32:05,042
It gets light.

796
00:32:05,042 --> 00:32:08,042
It gets light and then what?

797
00:32:08,042 --> 00:32:09,042
It turns dark.

798
00:32:09,042 --> 00:32:11,042
Six and a half thousand years.

799
00:32:11,042 --> 00:32:14,042
See, it's not likely to change.

800
00:32:14,042 --> 00:32:18,042
And we're not to be startled by that.

801
00:32:18,042 --> 00:32:20,042
If the sun goes down, the guy says, what's happened?

802
00:32:20,042 --> 00:32:21,042
What's happened?

803
00:32:21,042 --> 00:32:23,042
It means he hasn't been here long, I guess, right?

804
00:32:23,042 --> 00:32:27,042
It always goes down about this time.

805
00:32:27,042 --> 00:32:28,042
The guy says, well, I don't like that arrangement.

806
00:32:28,042 --> 00:32:31,042
